WEBVTT

cue0032431
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Shalom.

cue0032432
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:41.060
we're going to have a great night. And I tell you why. Because what is particularly unique

cue0032433
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:49.440
about the Sepharadi tradition is that it's terribly, terribly old and it's terribly,

cue0032434
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:59.920
terribly contemporary. Sephardim had a special experience. Yesterday we spoke that Sephardim

cue0032435
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:11.260
is the combination, the juncture between West and East. And because of this, in a sense,

cue0032436
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:21.600
we understood society, the political organizations of people, in a special way. And I want to

cue0032437
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:32.220
to speak today about the concept of law, the concept of a nation, what does it mean to

cue0032438
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:41.460
be a member of the Jewish nation, alright? And the implications of those ideas. I'm going

cue0032439
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:58.960
So to begin with a point of history, when 23 Jews came to this United States in 1640

cue0032440
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:10.560
and they asked permission from the government of New Amsterdam to settle, they didn't say

cue0032441
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:23.320
were 23 Jews, but they asked permission to settle in name of the Jewish nation. What

cue0032442
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:32.160
is terribly important for Sefer Ha'adim is that we are not just a religion. Of course

cue0032443
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:42.920
believe in God and have the whole Tora and the misva. But Judaism has also laws and

cue0032444
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:52.380
organizations that are considered political. Another point of history. When Napoleon entered

cue0032445
00:02:52.380 --> 00:03:05.540
Holland and he offered citizenship to some of the Jews there, the Jews refused to accept

cue0032446
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:14.840
citizenship. Why? Because they say, we as Jews, we are in Galut. What do you mean we

cue0032447
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:26.720
are in Galut, we are in exile. We hope one day to settle in Eretz Israel. We are members

cue0032448
00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:37.620
of the Jewish nation. If I accept your citizenship, Napoleon, means that I am no longer in Galut.

cue0032449
00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.680
If I'm not going to be in Galut, how could I have a Mashiah?

cue0032450
00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.900
You cannot be a French citizen and also in Galut.

cue0032451
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.760
There are two mutually exclusive concepts.

cue0032452
00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:57.380
So for the Sefer Adi, there is of course religion,

cue0032453
00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:04.820
but there is a whole community of Jews, no matter where they are,

cue0032454
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:19.060
no matter what the custom happens to be, but we are all part, Sephardim, Ashkenaz, Italians, Yemenites, we are the Jewish nation.

cue0032455
00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:26.380
What does it mean exactly to be a member of the Jewish nation? That is what I am going to speak today.

cue0032456
00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:37.380
Let me begin with a simple, simple idea. In the Western world, of course also the Orient,

cue0032457
00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:50.380
law, a judiciary system, a legal system, presupposes that there is a government, you have an authority,

cue0032458
00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:55.600
and the authority establishes a legal system.

cue0032459
00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:58.460
You begin with authority,

cue0032460
00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:05.660
and the authority develops the legal system.

cue0032461
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.660
Because you have authority, you have law.

cue0032462
00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:14.900
In the Sepharadi tradition, this is not the case.

cue0032463
00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.840
You're going to see the implications.

cue0032464
00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:24.840
In the Sepharadi tradition, because we have law, we could have a government. The beginning

cue0032465
00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:32.760
of the beginning is the covenant, the Berit, that all the Jewish people contracted at the

cue0032466
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:46.480
foot of Mount Sinai. We have a contract. This contract is a contract that is a contract

cue0032467
00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.600
created the Jewish people.

cue0032468
00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:56.880
The contract is the basis of everything.

cue0032469
00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.840
And because we have a contract,

cue0032470
00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:01.940
and the contract is the Tora,

cue0032471
00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.120
this contract not only unites us all,

cue0032472
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:12.040
but is the basis of all government.

cue0032473
00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:14.420
And here comes something,

cue0032474
00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.760
which is the way, in the Sepharadi tradition,

cue0032475
00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.480
they look into the Bible

cue0032476
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:25.700
and they describe the Bible as

cue0032477
00:06:25.700 --> 00:06:30.040
the constitution of Israel. The Bible,

cue0032478
00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:32.320
the Tora, of course is religious.

cue0032479
00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:37.920
Of course comes from God. But the

cue0032480
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:41.880
Tora is described as the constitution

cue0032481
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:44.520
of the Jewish people.

cue0032482
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:56.500
It is the basis of a nation. Now one point, we have to go further. We have in this country,

cue0032483
00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:03.480
we are blessed with a constitution, and many other countries. Now a constitution needs

cue0032484
00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:13.160
to be interpreted. In this country, only the Supreme Court could interpret the constitution.

cue0032485
00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:33.440
Of course a scholar could say this passage means A or B or C, but it becomes law not when a professor, a young professor, a Harvard professor of law explains a passage in the Constitution,

cue0032486
00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:47.620
but when the Supreme Court interprets it, and the Supreme Court says this is what the Constitution means.

cue0032487
00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:52.600
and that's why for the Sepharadi people

cue0032488
00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:56.060
the Bedin

cue0032489
00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:58.600
the Sanhedrin

cue0032490
00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:02.240
of Israel

cue0032491
00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.740
and only the Sanhedrin of Israel

cue0032492
00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:07.400
has the authority

cue0032493
00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.480
to interpret

cue0032494
00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:11.040
the Tora

cue0032495
00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.280
the Tora

cue0032496
00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.020
yes we could read it and we're going to that

cue0032497
00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:22.960
anyway we want it but it becomes law

cue0032498
00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:33.580
because the bedin of the Jewish people interpreted as such and now you're going

cue0032499
00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:38.620
to see how everything in the Jewish tradition is going to become very very

cue0032500
00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:51.460
logical. How long the Jewish Bedin lasted? It's important to a question. In the

cue0032501
00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:58.620
Jewish tradition, of course, the Second Temple was destroyed. We didn't have a

cue0032502
00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:08.620
temple. We could not bring sacrifices, but we had a Bedin. And the Bedin lasted

cue0032503
00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:13.980
until the end of the Talmudic period,

cue0032504
00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.260
year 499.

cue0032505
00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:20.940
Now we are going to understand the theory of Halakha.

cue0032506
00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.000
After 499,

cue0032507
00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:32.120
the Jewish people start scattering all over the world,

cue0032508
00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:38.580
and you cannot have a supreme court of Israel

cue0032509
00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.860
if you don't have the people of Israel.

cue0032510
00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:52.900
Democracy means that the Bedin of the nation of Israel

cue0032511
00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:56.760
and the nation of Israel are together.

cue0032512
00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:00.740
You cannot have Bedin in one country,

cue0032513
00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:04.280
that time it was Babylonia,

cue0032514
00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:09.180
and the people scattering in Italy, Germany, France, North Africa, and so on.

cue0032515
00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:18.480
So that's why Rambam says the Talmud is the last national beddin of Israel.

cue0032516
00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:22.920
And that is why things make sense.

cue0032517
00:10:24.940 --> 00:10:30.020
The interpretation of the Talmud is binding.

cue0032518
00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:31.960
Why?

cue0032519
00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:40.520
for the same reason that the interpretation of the Constitution by the Supreme Court of America is binding.

cue0032520
00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:53.980
So in the Sepharadi tradition, they saw themselves as a nation with the Supreme Court,

cue0032521
00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:59.380
our Supreme Court, the Sanhedrin, the Bedin, and with the law.

cue0032522
00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:03.320
and now we're going to go a little bit further

cue0032523
00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.780
we have a law

cue0032524
00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:12.520
but everybody today will no longer have a Supreme Court

cue0032525
00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:14.980
we no longer have a Supreme Court

cue0032526
00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:17.640
that law is very nice

cue0032527
00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:20.500
but you have different scholars

cue0032528
00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:25.020
and different scholars may interpret

cue0032529
00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.540
the same passage differently

cue0032530
00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:39.140
Here comes something terribly important in Sepharadi tradition. A community. Sepharadi, Ashkenaz,

cue0032531
00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:48.940
Italian, Yemenite, Persian, what have you. They have the right to interpret those laws of the Talmud

cue0032532
00:11:48.940 --> 00:12:01.840
according to their own tradition. So we are a nation, we are a nation with a common legal

cue0032533
00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:13.540
system, but the legal system does not choke you. People in their own locales, rabbis in their own

cue0032534
00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:22.900
locales could take the same passage and say no no we don't interpret it this way we interpret

cue0032535
00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:34.120
it differently there is unity but this unity allows for variety a great haqqan I love him

cue0032536
00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.160
Rabbi Yishael and Moshe Hazzan

cue0032537
00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:39.280
it is a beautiful metaphor

cue0032538
00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:40.460
about the Jewish people

cue0032539
00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:42.440
a tree

cue0032540
00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:47.020
with many branches

cue0032541
00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:50.320
same roots

cue0032542
00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:54.800
but branches have grown

cue0032543
00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:57.280
a little bit in different directions

cue0032544
00:12:57.280 --> 00:13:00.800
we all come from the same roots

cue0032545
00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:03.780
we all grow

cue0032546
00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:07.200
because of the same trunk.

cue0032547
00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:13.620
Every branch is a legitimate branch.

cue0032548
00:13:14.860 --> 00:13:17.720
And are we going to cut, if we cut any of the branches,

cue0032549
00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.340
that would affect the whole tree.

cue0032550
00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.640
One of the most beautiful ideas is that by being a nation,

cue0032551
00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:29.420
we have common interests,

cue0032552
00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:34.780
but also the point of particularities.

cue0032553
00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.880
We don't have to be totally homogeneous.

cue0032554
00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.200
And that's why it's so beautiful within this tradition

cue0032555
00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:43.720
that there are different songs,

cue0032556
00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.180
different ways how to pray.

cue0032557
00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:49.400
Some people, they would stand up in one prayer,

cue0032558
00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:50.280
others would sit down.

cue0032559
00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.700
Some people would put their feet in this way,

cue0032560
00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.100
the other, the other way.

cue0032561
00:13:56.100 --> 00:14:00.720
but we all are from the same tree

cue0032562
00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:06.220
and now I'm going to say something more

cue0032563
00:14:06.220 --> 00:14:07.800
about the Sepharadi tradition

cue0032564
00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:14.180
because we did not see ourselves only as

cue0032565
00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:15.140
a religion

cue0032566
00:14:15.140 --> 00:14:21.440
dealing with theological concepts

cue0032567
00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:24.600
who is God, where is God, what is God

cue0032568
00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:35.480
that we put our accent in the most important question that any human being could ask and

cue0032569
00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:48.360
that any human being could answer. What am I going to do with my life? How should I live?

cue0032570
00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:56.520
The whole notion of halakha that is so precious in the Sepharadi tradition, what the law wants

cue0032571
00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:07.380
me to do, how I should behave, its premise and the idea that the most important question

cue0032572
00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:13.300
you, you, me, everybody should ask is, how am I going to live?

cue0032573
00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:28.300
Therefore we see the Tora not only as a set of values, it has values, not only as a set of philosophy,

cue0032574
00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:34.300
but as a guide for present living.

cue0032575
00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:40.300
The Tora comes to ask a question and to give an answer to the question.

cue0032576
00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:47.440
the question is what am I going to do now and the Tora comes to answer you

cue0032577
00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:56.680
should do now this it's different than seeing the Tora some rational

cue0032578
00:15:56.680 --> 00:16:06.640
theological assistant now I want to talk a little bit about mysticism because in

cue0032579
00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:14.640
In the Sepharadi tradition, we developed three models how to be a Jew.

cue0032580
00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:17.140
There are three models of how to be a Jew.

cue0032581
00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:25.240
Anybody who wants to be a Jew today has only one of these three models.

cue0032582
00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:31.140
There is no other way how to be a Jew except one of these three models.

cue0032583
00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:34.640
I challenge anybody to find me a fourth model.

cue0032584
00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:40.640
Three models were manufactured in the Sephantic tradition.

cue0032585
00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:47.240
One, the halakha model, followed by halakha,

cue0032586
00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:54.440
my manners, Mishne Tora, Shohar Haruch, Manan Yosef Qaro,

cue0032587
00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:58.340
the typical Orthodox view.

cue0032588
00:16:58.340 --> 00:17:02.060
I live according to a code.

cue0032589
00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:11.940
I am a regimented Jew who models his life according to Jewish law.

cue0032590
00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:14.760
That is one model.

cue0032591
00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:26.960
One of the great things developed in Sefarad is mysticism, the mystical tradition.

cue0032592
00:17:26.960 --> 00:17:34.720
What is the mystical tradition? The mystical tradition asks the same question and gives

cue0032593
00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:41.380
the same answer in different ways. Listen to this. We said that the halakha come to

cue0032594
00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:48.520
ask the question, what am I going to do with my life? Of course, there is a common aspect,

cue0032595
00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:57.280
are all the same. Jewish mysticism come to ask another question, very similar. Each of

cue0032596
00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:07.820
us happens to be different. There was no person like you, or like you, or like you from the

cue0032597
00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:13.760
beginning of history, and there will never be another like you to the end of history.

cue0032598
00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:25.360
There are specific circumstances that allow us to do certain things and prevent us from doing others.

cue0032599
00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:40.260
The basis of Jewish mysticism is the idea that there were never two individuals identical.

cue0032600
00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:53.320
Each person has certain qualities that make him or her totally different than others.

cue0032601
00:18:53.320 --> 00:19:03.440
Therefore, Jewish mysticism comes to answer the question, why I am here now?

cue0032602
00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:07.700
What is the purpose of my life?

cue0032603
00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:17.220
Because my father was A, and my mother was B, and I was born in that country, and I have this and that,

cue0032604
00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:22.740
I could do certain things that nobody else can do.

cue0032605
00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:42.380
I have certain opportunities given to me that they will never be given to any other individual in history, nor anybody in the past had the same circumstances that I have right now here.

cue0032606
00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:58.060
mysticism comes to help you find the uniqueness in you. There's a beautiful, beautiful metaphor

cue0032607
00:19:58.060 --> 00:20:04.820
about Jewish mysticism. And this metaphor was first deciphered by one of the greatest

cue0032608
00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:10.980
geniuses in Jewish history, Rabbi Shelomo Ibn Gabirol from Malaga. If you go to Malaga,

cue0032609
00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:20.520
It's very nice for the winter. It's great. You could swim there. It's the Miami of the Mediterranean. All right?

cue0032610
00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:40.200
You remember that when the patriarch Jacob left home and went north to Laban, to his uncle Laban, to fetch a wife, and then he slept, and he had a dream.

cue0032611
00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:44.540
and the dream was a ladder

cue0032612
00:20:44.540 --> 00:20:49.540
firm on the ground

cue0032613
00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:53.540
going up to heaven.

cue0032614
00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:57.940
Do you remember this story in the Bible?

cue0032615
00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:01.340
And there the patriarch Jacob

cue0032616
00:21:01.340 --> 00:21:02.880
sees angels

cue0032617
00:21:02.880 --> 00:21:10.020
The angels are going up

cue0032618
00:21:10.020 --> 00:21:12.320
and down.

cue0032619
00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:14.620
And Rabbi Shalom

cue0032620
00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:15.520
I mean, God be Lord,

cue0032621
00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:17.660
that's a question

cue0032622
00:21:17.660 --> 00:21:18.960
difficult to answer.

cue0032623
00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:21.120
What kind of angels are these?

cue0032624
00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:24.180
Angels belong in heaven.

cue0032625
00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:26.000
So the scripture,

cue0032626
00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:26.960
the Tora should say,

cue0032627
00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:29.380
and the angels come down

cue0032628
00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:33.440
and then go up.

cue0032629
00:21:35.860 --> 00:21:38.340
How could the angels go up

cue0032630
00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:58.780
and done? The answer is Jewish mysticism. The angels are not in heaven. The angels happen to be you and she and her and you and you and even me and him.

cue0032631
00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:06.220
The angels are humans on planet earth.

cue0032632
00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:09.340
We go up the ladder.

cue0032633
00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:12.600
We go up closer to heaven.

cue0032634
00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:20.020
Our life, when lived properly, elevates us.

cue0032635
00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:27.020
Yes, today we should understand more than yesterday.

cue0032636
00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:32.360
And tomorrow we should be a little bit better than today.

cue0032637
00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:38.260
The latter is human life.

cue0032638
00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:45.360
Human life allows us to grow and grow and grow.

cue0032639
00:22:45.360 --> 00:22:53.860
But here's the difference between the Jewish mystic, and I have only praised for them,

cue0032640
00:22:53.860 --> 00:23:03.380
I don't share the philosophy and the Buddhist. This is the difference. The Buddhist reaches

cue0032641
00:23:03.380 --> 00:23:15.520
Nirvana and remains in Nirvana. It's there. The Jewish mystic, and the Jewish mystic could

cue0032642
00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:21.520
Could be you, could be you, could be us.

cue0032643
00:23:21.520 --> 00:23:29.700
When we reach the top of the ladder, we cannot stay there.

cue0032644
00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:39.780
We have to bring that vision down and share it with others.

cue0032645
00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:44.760
All what the Zohar, all what the qabbala, all what the Ahari, they come to teach you

cue0032646
00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:55.720
just this simple idea. You find yourself, you grow, you grasp something unique at the

cue0032647
00:23:55.720 --> 00:24:11.520
Divine, but don't stay up there. The Tumar'aq is the one who comes down, he understood something

cue0032648
